From: Mirdispo contributors
Subject: Start a cast at a volume somebody would not reach to turn down

The output a cast captures through is made when the cast starts, and a new
one starts at full volume. The receiver at the other end is usually a
television whose own volume was set for whatever it last played. Full from
both ends arrives loud, and the first second of a cast is spent reaching for
a remote.

It starts at seven tenths instead. Being too quiet is the easier mistake to
correct: turning something up is a thing people do without minding, and being
startled is not. It appears in the sound settings like any other output and
can be set to whatever suits, and the setting is per cast rather than
remembered, since the next receiver may be a different television in a
different room.

diff -ruN a/src/nd-pulseaudio.c b/src/nd-pulseaudio.c
--- a/src/nd-pulseaudio.c
+++ b/src/nd-pulseaudio.c
@@ -199,6 +199,24 @@
 
   g_debug ("NdPulseaudio: Module loaded, we are ready to grab audio! ");
   self->null_module_idx = idx;
+
+  /* A new sink starts at full volume, and the receiver is usually a
+   * television whose own volume is set for whatever it was last playing.
+   * Full from both ends arrives loud enough to make somebody reach for a
+   * remote, which is a poor first second of a cast. Starting lower leaves
+   * room to be turned up, which is the direction a person would rather
+   * move; this sink appears in the sound settings like any other and can
+   * be set to whatever suits. */
+  {
+    g_autofree gchar *sink_name = g_strdup_printf (ND_PA_SINK "_%.8s", self->uuid);
+    pa_operation *volume_operation;
+    pa_cvolume volume;
+
+    pa_cvolume_set (&volume, 2, (pa_volume_t) (PA_VOLUME_NORM * 0.7));
+    volume_operation = pa_context_set_sink_volume_by_name (c, sink_name, &volume, NULL, NULL);
+    if (volume_operation)
+      pa_operation_unref (volume_operation);
+  }
   nd_pulseaudio_route_to_sink (self);
   return_idle_success (self->init_task);
   g_clear_object (&self->init_task);
